= Introduction

#note[ Emancipatory nature of the internet and reality ]
With the invention of the internet , many have viewed this technology as a potential means of social change. Unfortunately, this never materialized. Even though, the internet permits peer to peer communication, eventually the applications that were built on top of it were centrally controlled, by specific companies. The distribution of wealth in Society eventually led to the same distribution of control on the internet.


#note[The role of the app as a method of control]
If in the material world, the rules are enforced by the government , the police and the financial dependence of workers to capital for subsistence , in the digital world, it is the applications as the last point of control of the users. Can we then build applications that give freedom back to the users? that give agency and autonomy back to the communities? What would it take for the internet to reach its emancipatory potential?

#note[Need for understanding the whole cyberphysical system]
It is important to view applications as rules that either enable a group's actions or constrain them. If we consider this group as an system with a defined boundary to the environmnet, then we might say that it changes the dynamics of this system. The system though has dynamics that are determined by other factors as well, such as physical properties and relations. Thus in order to understand the effect of an application on a system, it is imperative to understand the whole system and all its dynamics, both those enabled by the digital tools and those determined by the material world.

#note[Specifically an understanding of the physical world, capitalism and revolution (in order to explain why we attempt to model the cyberphysical system)]
This eventually demands the construction of a theory and practice that incorporates an understanding of the physical world and an understanding of Society. Specifically, it demands an understanding of Capitalism and of the methods of liberation that preexisted the internet. How can anticapitalist and revolutionary practice affect the use and creation of digital tools and how will those tools enable new forms of resistance and transformation?

